<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Uhle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Max Uhle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Uhle was a German archaeologist whose work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> at the turn of the 20th century had a significant impact on the practice of South American archaeology. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and trained as a philologist, he took his doctorate at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1880, and in 1892 published, with B. von Grumbkow, the first in-depth scientific account of Tiwanaku. From 1896 he excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> and Mochica and Chimu sites, recovering about 9,000 artifacts, and built a chronology from their styles that Alfred Kroeber later advanced and that remains key to the dating of pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Peru. In 1917 he was the first to describe the Chinchorro mummies scientifically.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-uhle.jpg" alt="Max Uhle" width="244" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: public domain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uhle_max.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Uhle was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, on 25 March 1856. His interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> began while he was a curator at the Dresden Museum, and in 1888 his close friend Alphons Stübel, who had recently published an article on the history of Peruvian archaeology, urged him to concentrate on the region. His first journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, in 1892, took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> on behalf of the Konigliches Museum fur Völkerkunde in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. His partner on the Tiwanaku study was the photographer and engineer B. von Grumbkow. Uhle married Charlotte Grosse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, where he worked for several years at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His second expedition, from 1896, was sponsored by the American Exploration Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">University of Pennsylvania Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology</a>, with the patronage of Phoebe Hearst, mother of William Randolph Hearst. His report on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> was highly praised and is still used as a basic text of South American archaeology. At the coastal sites he recognised versions of Tiwanaku sculptural imagery on ceramics and textiles, and the finds he recovered, spanning more than 3,000 years, included Nazca pottery, shells, textiles, metals and objects of wood, feathers, bone and leather. He dated them chiefly by textile design, and those of the Mocha Valley by the sequence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ceramic styles.</p><p>He went on to work in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> and excavated the Emeryville shell-mound on San Francisco Bay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. He also took part in palaeontological digs: in 1926, with the palaeontologist Franz Spillmann, he excavated an almost complete mastodon skeleton about 12 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, together with obsidian and bone tools and about 150 potsherds. He died on 11 May 1944. The German-Peruvian Max Uhle School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a> bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Max Uhle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Max Uhle</b> excavated at the Huacas de Moche in 1899–1900, and Rafael Larco Hoyle, whose collection forms the Museo Larco in Lima, defined the culture and its phases.</blockquote><a href="/empire/moche">Moche · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Aspero on the coast was identified as early as 1905, the year <b>Max Uhle</b> also noted Caral without recognising its hills as pyramids.</blockquote><a href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation">Caral–Supe civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The term “trophy head”, coined by the archaeologist <b>Max Uhle</b>, comes from his reading of severed heads in ancient Peruvian art as trophies of war, though early Nazca pottery suggests that their first purpose may have been tied to farming.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nazca-culture">Nazca culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Lubliniec, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Max Uhle"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5732 5686h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5732 5686h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
